    "canonical_url": "https://www.defensenews.com/industry/techwatch/2026/10/12/lockheeds-new-pac-3-edge-interceptor-aims-for-agility/",
    "origin_publisher": "Defense News",
    "published_at": "2026-10-12T18:11:55.266000+00:00",
    "published_at_raw": "2026-10-12T18:11:55.266Z",
    "publication_precision": "TIMESTAMP",
    "acquired_at": "2026-10-12T19:35:17.615423+00:00",
    "available_at": "2026-10-12T19:35:17.615423+00:00",
    "body": "The latest iteration of Lockheed Martin’s PAC-3 family of missile interceptors is designed to bring extreme agility to bear against a diverse array of missile threats, Defense News learned.\nThe defense giant unveiled the PAC-3 Edge this Friday and debuted it at the Association of the U.S. Army’s annual meeting in Washington, D.C., today.\n“This missile is capable of being launched from the traditional M903 launcher. It’s capable of being launched from the containerized launchers that are being sought after domestically and internationally, as well as our HIMARS FLEX, which is the offense and defense configuration of our traditional HIMARS vehicle,” Jason Reynolds, vice president and general manager of Lockheed Martin Missiles and Fire Control Integrated Air and Missile Defense, told Defense News.\nLockheed in January announced a seven-year framework agreement to increase annual PAC-3 interceptor production from approximately 600 to 2,000.\nDesigned to defend against hypersonic and advanced missile threats, the unveiling of the PAC-3 Edge comes as the U.S. military’s reliance on costly interceptors against cheap munitions, particularly those deployed by Iran during Operation Epic Fury, has come under increased scrutiny over the past eight months.\nThe new PAC-3 Edge missile builds on the foundations of the PAC-3 MSE, Lockheed’s baseline missile, and pushes its abilities farther.\nThe Edge system is longer and features a larger diameter, increasing its range.\n“It also goes higher in altitude and has more agility. It has attitude control motors, which are located near the front of the missile and basically divert the missile in flight,” Reynolds explained. “This has additional attitude control motors that give it additional agility.”\nWhile the PAC-3 MSE uses a two-pulse solid rocket motor to speedily approach moving targets, the new system has multi-pulse motor designed by Northrop Grumman.\n“It has a third pulse to the motor, so that means the motor will fire three times during its flight, giving it additional agility to kill the target,” Reynolds said.\nThe weapon is designed not only for unprecedented maneuverability but to be easily integrated with both present and future missile defense networks. It is compatible with Northrop’s Integrated Battle Command System, or IBCS, a command-and-control network the Army uses to mesh air and missile defense sensors into a central hub.\nThe company intends to begin testing the PAC-3 Edge in 2027 and have it ready to deploy by 2031, although Lockheed aims to have it ready earlier, Reynolds added.\n“The U.S. Army’s timeline is 2031. We, Lockheed Martin, are trying to pull that in and deliver this capability sooner, so around the turn of the decade,” he said.\nAbout Zita Ballinger Fletcher\nZita Ballinger Fletcher previously served as editor of Military History Quarterly and Vietnam magazines and as the historian of the U.S. Drug Enforcement Administration. She holds an M.A. with distinction in military history.\nShare:\nMore In MilTech\nTrust between operators, CCAs starts with simulations: Former Air Force secretary\nSimulations and exercises will be key to boosting trust between operators and CCAs, according to former U.S. Air Force secretary Frank Kendall.\nCelebrating 40 years of Defense News\nSince 1986, Defense News has been the authoritative voice for the world's defense decision-makers.\nGuardsman learns to fly autonomous Black Hawk in less than an hour\nIn a training first, an Army National Guardsman used a tablet to control Sikorsky's optionally piloted Black Hawk and plan its task during an exercise.\nRecap: Highlights from the US Army’s 2025 conference in Washington\nThe Army continues to transform, with efforts focused on next-gen weaponry, long-range artillery, unmanned systems and much more.\nPentagon stages first ‘Top Drone’ school for operators to hone skills\nIndustry and military drone operators flew tethered and untethered first-person drones through a course designed to test endurance and maneuverability.",
